WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.620
What's up? Welcome to models and agents for beginners episode 20 for April 14th, 2026.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.040
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it. Let's go.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:15.940
So imagine you had a superpatient detective who could look through millions of lines of computer

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:21.960
code and find hidden problems that even expert humans missed for years. That is basically what happened

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:27.660
with Anthropics new model called Mythos. Instead of just chatting or writing essays like many AI tools,

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:33.960
Mythos acted like an autonomous security researcher. It scanned complex programs spotted hidden

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.540
weaknesses and even wrote working attacks to prove the problems were real. Think of Mythos like

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:45.380
the world's most patient bug detective. Normal human testers might spend weeks looking through millions

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:50.900
of lines of code with special tools. Mythos does something smarter. It tries thousands of different

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:57.200
approaches learns from what fails and chains tiny clues together until it cracks open a brand new

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.940
discovery. For example, it found a flaw in open BSD, which is a super secure operating system

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:07.900
trusted for decades by noticing two small math errors that no one had connected before.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:14.900
It also uncovered a 16-year-old bug in FFMPEG, the free software that handles video and audio on

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:21.420
millions of devices even though experts had tested it endlessly. This is a big deal for everyday life

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:26.860
because almost everything we do online like watching TikTok sending messages or playing games

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:31.240
runs on software that could have hidden weaknesses. If bad actors find those first,

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:36.960
they can steal information or break things. Mythos shows A, I can help find those problems early,

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:42.980
potentially saving companies and users from expensive hacks. The report even shares real costs.

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:49.660
Discovering some serious issues cost only $10,000 to $20,000 total compared to millions a company

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:55.220
might lose from one big breach. For use specifically, this news is exciting and a little bit challenging.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:02:01.600
It means the cybersecurity world is changing fast. Jobs that only follow checklists or run basic

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:08.460
scans might shrink, but roles that combine deep human judgment with AI skills will grow. The report

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:13.360
is honest about limits too. Mythos still needs humans to judge which findings matter and to handle

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.840
tricky real-world contexts like laws or business risks. So the humans who learn to work alongside AI

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.960
will be the most valuable. You can explore this yourself right now without any special equipment.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:28.400
Go read the actual report. It is public. Start with the summary sections, then look at the examples

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.300
of bugs it found. Ask yourself which of these feels most surprising. That simple habit of reading

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:36.980
primary sources is exactly what the researchers recommend for staying ahead.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:42.040
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, The Deep Dive. Today we are going to explain exactly how

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:47.380
AI finds brand new security bugs. It is not just searching a list and I promise we will build this

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:51.820
from something you already know. You know how when you lose something in your room you do not just

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:56.760
look in the same three spots over and over. You start by checking obvious places then you notice wait.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:01.680
The gap between my bed and the wall is weird and that clue leads you to move the bed which reveals

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:07.380
another clue under it. That is basically how the smartest AI security tools are starting to work.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:14.220
Step one. Instead of memorizing a checklist of known bad patterns like a robot only looking for red

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:20.160
socks, the AI loads up a piece of real software and just watches how every tiny part talks to every

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.500
other part at the same time. It is observing the living structure not hunting for keywords.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:32.380
Step two. It spots mismatches places where the code says this is safe in one spot but then does something

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:37.740
risky a few lines later. These mismatches are like tiny cracks between what the program promises and

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:43.320
what it actually does. Step three. The AI does not stop there. It looks at its own discoveries and

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:49.180
asks do all these cracks have something in common. In the mythos case it noticed every bug involved a

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:54.800
gap in time between checking something and using it. That pattern became a brand new category called

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:01.640
temporal trust gaps which basically means the software trusted its own earlier check even though the

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:08.700
world changed in between. And that is basically what advanced AI bug hunting is doing now. It is not

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:15.520
just matching patterns from a textbook. It is watching noticing contradictions then noticing patterns

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:20.440
across the contradictions. So next time someone says AI found a brand new vulnerability class,

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:26.180
you can tell them it is basically like a super curious detective who keeps asking but why are all

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:31.900
these clues shaped the same way? Not so mysterious after all, right? Let us move on to some cool stuff

